# Your economics engine still contains dangerous fallback assumptions

This is the biggest pure-code issue in the newest work.

`calculateEconomics()` currently falls back to:

```ts
cachedRate =
  inputPrice * 0.2;
```

if no cached rate exists.

It also falls back to:

```ts
requestsPerMonth = 30_000;
```

Then the CLI adds even more guesses:

```ts
output_per_1m:
  output?.value ?? input.value * 3

requests_per_month:
  requests?.value ?? 30000

provider_usage_usd:
  providerUsage?.value ?? 60
```

This is unacceptable for the submission you're trying to pitch.

If a fact is absent:

```text
UNKNOWN
```

must mean unknown.

Not:

```text
probably 3×
probably 30K
probably $60
probably cache = 20%
```

### Change immediately

```ts
if (!outputPrice)
  return notComputable("missing_output_price");

if (!requestsPerMonth)
  return notComputable("missing_request_limit");

if (cacheTokens > 0 && cachedRate == null)
  return notComputable("missing_cache_price");
```

Your whole selling point is trustworthy live economics.

Never fabricate missing economics.

